TASK 1
SELECT region,state,city,
sum(sales_amount) as sales_total
FROM ex_customers c join ex_orders o 
	ON c.customer_id=o.customer_id
	JOIN ex_transactions t 
	ON t.Order_ID=o.order_id
	JOIN ex_locations l
	ON l.postal_code=c.postal_code
GROUP BY ROLLUP(region,state,city);

TASK 2
SELECT region,state,city,
sum(sales_amount) as sales_total
FROM ex_customers c join ex_orders o 
	ON c.customer_id=o.customer_id
	JOIN ex_transactions t 
	ON t.Order_ID=o.order_id
	JOIN ex_locations l
	ON l.postal_code=c.postal_code
GROUP BY CUBE(city,state,region);

TASK 3
SELECT region,state,city,
sum(sales_amount) as sales_total
FROM ex_customers c join ex_orders o 
	ON c.customer_id=o.customer_id
	JOIN ex_transactions t 
	ON t.Order_ID=o.order_id
	JOIN ex_locations l
	ON l.postal_code=c.postal_code
GROUP BY grouping sets((region,state,city),(region,state),(region),());

TASK 4
When we want to uncover hidden patterns in the data and do exploratory analysis
Rollup is used when there is a clear hierarchy in the data and we want hierarchical aggregations.

TASK 5
In cube if there are k columns to group by, 2^k grouping sets are made. It generates all possible combinations of grouping columns.

Grouping sets gives us absolute control over which subsets of grouping columns we want.
It avoids calculating unneeded subtotals, which saves heavy database engine resources compared to a full cube.
